CVE-2023-38831 (WinRAR Remote Code Execution)
RARLAB WinRAR before 6.23 allows attackers to execute arbitrary code when a user attempts to view a benign file within a ZIP archive. 
The issue occurs because a ZIP archive may include a benign file (such as an ordinary .JPG file) and also a folder that has the same name as the benign file, and the contents of the folder (which may include executable content) are processed during an attempt to access only the benign file. 
This was exploited in the wild in April through October 2023.

According to the National Vulnerability Database (NVD), this vulnerability allows attackers to execute malicious code when a user attempts to open an ordinary-looking file inside a specially crafted ZIP archive. 
The archive contains a file and a folder with the same name, which can cause WinRAR to process malicious content.
Successful exploitation could lead to unauthorized access, data theft, or modification of files. The vulnerability was exploited in real-world attacks in 2023.

Patch and Remediation
The vulnerability was fixed in WinRAR version 6.23, released on August 2, 2023.
Remediation steps:
1. Identify systems running vulnerable WinRAR versions.
2. Update WinRAR to the latest available version.
3. Avoid opening suspicious ZIP archives from untrusted sources.
4. Scan systems for possible malware infections.
5. Investigate suspicious activity on potentially affected computers.
The official update is available on the WinRAR Download Page.

 Severity Assessment

The NVD assigns CVE-2023-38831 a CVSS 3.1 score of 7.8 (High). The score indicates that successful exploitation could seriously affect a system’s confidentiality, integrity, and availability. 
User interaction is required, but the attacker does not need existing privileges.
CVSS Vector: CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H

